S_n: total number of successes in n Bernoulli trials;
p: probability of success on a single trial;
q=1-p: probability of failure;
np: expected number of successes;
\sqrt{npq}: standard deviation of the binomial distribution;
\frac{S_n-np}{\sqrt{npq}}: standardized version of S_n;
\phi(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-x^2/2}: standard normal density;
NA(a,b)=\int_a^b\phi(x)\,dx: area under the standard normal density from a to b.

$\mathbf{P}$ – transition matrix of a regular Markov chain;
$\mathbf{W}$ – limit of $\mathbf{P}^{n}$, a matrix with identical rows equal to the unique fixed probability row vector $\mathbf{w}$;
$\mathbf{w}$ – stationary distribution (row vector) satisfying $\mathbf{w}\mathbf{P}=\mathbf{w}$;
$M_{n}$ – maximum component of $\mathbf{P}^{n}\mathbf{y}$;
$m_{n}$ – minimum component of $\mathbf{P}^{n}\mathbf{y}$;
$d$ – smallest entry of $\mathbf{P}$;
$r$ – number of states;
$\mathbf{y}$ – arbitrary column vector;
$\mathbf{I}$ – identity matrix.

Theorem 5.1 provides the density of a random variable obtained by applying a monotone transformation to a continuous random variable, giving the general formula 

[
    f_{g(X)}(y)=sum_{x:g(x)=y} rac{f_X(x)}{|g'(x)|}
] 

For the specific case of squaring a symmetric continuous variable, it simplifies to 

[
    f_{X^2}(r)= egin{cases}	frac{1}{2sqrt r}, igl(f_X(sqrt r)+f_X(-sqrt r) igr), & r>0,\[4pt]0, & rle0.
end{cases}

(b(n,p,j)= inom{n}{j}p^{j}q^{,n-j}) denotes the binomial probability mass function;
(q=1-p);
(x=(j-np)/sqrt{npq}) is the standardized deviation of the integer outcome;
(phi(x)=(1/sqrt{2pi})e^{-x^{2}/2}) is the standard normal density function;
(sqrt{npq},b(n,p,j)) is the scaled binomial probability used in the approximation.

(m): number of equally likely outcomes per trial; 
(k): desired length of a consecutive run of a particular outcome; 
(E): expected number of independent trials until the first occurrence of a run of length (k); 
States ({1,2,dots ,k}) in the Markov chain represent the current length of the run; 
Absorbing state (k) corresponds to having achieved the run of length (k).

p_A – probability that pattern A occurs before pattern B;
 p_B – probability that pattern B occurs before pattern A (p_B = 1 – p_A);
 AA – expected waiting time (or “pay‑off”) for pattern A when played alone;
 BB – expected waiting time for pattern B when played alone;
 AB – expected amount earned by a gambler who arrives during an occurrence of A and then waits until B occurs;
 BA – analogous quantity when the gambler arrives during B and waits for A;
 E(T^{A or B}) – expected number of tosses until either A or B appears for the first time.

$P$ – transition matrix of the ergodic chain;
$W$ – matrix whose rows are the stationary row vector $w$;
$w$ – stationary row probability vector satisfying $wP=w$ and $wW=w$;
$I$ – identity matrix;
$Z=(I-P+W)^{-1}$ – fundamental matrix;
$c$ – column vector of all ones;
$M$ – mean first‑passage matrix with entries $m_{ij}$;
$z_{ij}$ – $(i,j)$ entry of $Z$;
$r_j=1/w_j$ – reciprocal of the $j$‑th stationary probability.

$u_{2m}$ – probability of returning to the origin at time $2m$;
$f_{2m}$ – probability of a first return to the origin at time $2m$;
$U(x)=sum_{m=0}^infty u_{2m}x^m$ – ordinary generating function of the return probabilities;
$F(x)=sum_{m=0}^infty f_{2m}x^m$ – ordinary generating function of the first–return probabilities;
$ inom{2m}{m}$ – central binomial coefficient.

The probability that a simple symmetric random walk of length $2m$ has no equalization (i.e., never returns to the origin after time 0) is 
$$P(	ext{no equalization in }2m)=u_{2m}
= rac{ inom{2m}{m}}{2^{2m}},. $$

T denotes the number of trials until the first success;
 p is the probability of success on each trial;
 q=1-p;
 The probability mass function is P(T=n)=q^{,n-1}p.

S – total number of items in the population;
D – number of defective (or “success”) items in the population;
s – size of the drawn sample;
d – number of defectives observed in the sample;
N – often used for the population size in later problems;
k – number of successes in the population in the limit formulation;
n – sample size in the limit formulation.

R – the matrix of transition probabilities from transient states to absorbing states;
Q – the sub‑matrix of transitions among transient states;
N=(I-Q)^{-1} – the fundamental matrix, giving expected numbers of visits to transient states;
B=N\,R – the matrix of absorption probabilities;
t – the column vector of expected times to absorption, obtained as t=N\mathbf{1};
c – the column vector of ones used in the definition of t.
